spark.conf.set(
    "spark.databricks.delta.schema.autoMerge.enabled",
    "true"
)

This is a Spark session-level configuration for Delta schema evolution.

1. DataFrame API — df.write / df.writeStream ✅
df.write \
  .format("delta") \
  .mode("append") \
  .save("/delta/customers")

 Overwrite

This is a different concept.

df.write \
  .format("delta") \
  .mode("overwrite") \   # Replace the existing table's data with the incoming data.
  .option("overwriteSchema", "true") \ # Replace the data AND replace the target schema with the incoming DataFrame schema.Use in combination with mode.
  .save("/delta/customers") 


2. Delta API — DeltaTable.merge() ✅

# 1. Databricks SQL APIDatabricks SQL enforces schemas by default. To evolve schemas via SQL, you must explicitly use specific clauses like WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION 
# or run structural ALTER statements.

-- ==========================================
-- SCHEMA ENFORCEMENT (Default Behavior)
-- ==========================================
-- This write will FAIL if source_table contains a column (e.g., 'phone') not in target_table
INSERT INTO target_table 
SELECT * FROM source_table;

-- ==========================================
-- SCHEMA EVOLUTION EXAMPLES
-- ==========================================

-- Example A: Evolution via MERGE (Upsert)
MERGE WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO target_table t
USING source_table s
ON t.id = s.id
WHEN MATCHED THEN
  UPDATE SET *
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
  INSERT *;

-- Example B: Evolution via INSERT INTO (Append)
INSERT WITH SCHEMA EVOLUTION INTO target_table
SELECT * FROM source_table;

-- Example C: Manual Evolution via DDL
ALTER TABLE target_table ADD COLUMNS (phone STRING, discount DOUBLE);

2. DataFrame API (df.write)The standard Spark DataFrame API uses table configuration or explicit options to control write safety.
# Assume 'df' has a new column 'email' that doesn't exist in the target table

# ==========================================
-- SCHEMA ENFORCEMENT (Default Behavior)
# ==========================================
# This will throw an AnalysisException because 'email' is an unexpected column
df.write \
  .format("delta") \
  .mode("append") \
  .saveAsTable("customers")

# ==========================================
-- SCHEMA EVOLUTION EXAMPLES
# ==========================================

# Example A: Evolution on Append (Adds 'email' column to target)
df.write \
  .format("delta") \
  .mode("append") \
  .option("mergeSchema", "true") \
  .saveAsTable("customers")

# Example B: Complete Schema Overwrite (Replaces old schema entirely)
df.write \
  .format("delta") \
  .mode("overwrite") \
  .option("overwriteSchema", "true") \
  .saveAsTable("customers")

3. Delta Table API (DeltaTable)The programmatic Delta Table API is primarily used for complex file updates and programmatic merges using Python/Scala builders.
from delta.tables import DeltaTable

# ==========================================
-- SCHEMA ENFORCEMENT (Default Behavior)
# ==========================================
# This will fail if source_df contains columns missing from the target table
target = DeltaTable.forName(spark, "customers")

(target.alias("t")
  .merge(source_df.alias("s"), "t.id = s.id")
  .whenMatchedUpdateAll()
  .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()
  .execute())

# ==========================================
-- SCHEMA EVOLUTION EXAMPLES
# ==========================================
# Explicitly chained option allows the upsert to update the target table structure
(target.alias("t")
  .merge(source_df.alias("s"), "t.id = s.id")
  .withSchemaEvolution()       # <-- Triggers schema evolution
  .whenMatchedUpdateAll()
  .whenNotMatchedInsertAll()
  .whenNotMatchedBySourceDelete()    # target missing --> DELETE
  .execute())

4. Auto Loader API (cloudFiles)Auto Loader operates at the streaming reader layer. Instead of failing the write, it monitors files in cloud storage and handles schema variance based on your evolution mode configuration.
# ==========================================
-- SCHEMA ENFORCEMENT (Fail or Safe Drop)
# ==========================================
df_enforce = (spark.readStream
  .format("cloudFiles")
  .option("cloudFiles.format", "json")
  .option("cloudFiles.schemaLocation", "/mnt/telemetry/schemas/enforced")
  .option("cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode", "failOnNewColumns") # <-- Fails stream on structural change
  .load("/mnt/telemetry/raw_incoming/"))

# ==========================================
-- SCHEMA EVOLUTION (Automatic Column Merging)
# ==========================================
df_evolve = (spark.readStream
  .format("cloudFiles")
  .option("cloudFiles.format", "json")
  .option("cloudFiles.schemaLocation", "/mnt/telemetry/schemas/evolved")
  # addNewColumns tells Auto Loader to dynamically update the tracked schema directory
  .option("cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode", "addNewColumns") 
#   failOnNewColumns
#   .option(
#     "cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode",
#     "failOnNewColumns"
# )
# rescue
# .option(
#     "cloudFiles.schemaEvolutionMode",
#     "rescue"
# )
  .load("/mnt/telemetry/raw_incoming/"))

# Streaming output automatically propagates evolved schemas down to the Delta table
query = (df_evolve.writeStream
  .format("delta")
  .option("checkpointLocation", "/mnt/telemetry/checkpoints/evolved")
  .option(
        "mergeSchema",
        "true"
    )
    .outputMode("append")
  # Writes to catalog table
  .toTable("telemetry_target"))   
  # OR write to location
  # .start("/delta/customers")


# .stop() is primarily a Structured Streaming DataFrame API method.
# query.stop()       # Stop the stream
# query.awaitTermination()  # Wait until stream terminates
# query.isActive     # Check whether stream is running
# query.status       # Current status
# query.exception()  # Streaming exception, if any

# .trigger() is a Structured Streaming writeStream concept,
1. Default Trigger
Code
query = (
    df.writeStream
      .format("delta")
      .option("checkpointLocation", "/checkpoints/customers")
      .toTable("silver.customers")
)
Important

We don't write .trigger() at all.

# No .trigger()

Spark uses the normal Structured Streaming micro-batch execution model and processes available data as the query runs.

2. Processing-Time Trigger
Code
query = (
    df.writeStream
      .format("delta")
      .option("checkpointLocation", "/checkpoints/customers")
      .trigger(processingTime="5 minutes")
      .toTable("silver.customers")
)
Meaning

Spark attempts to execute a micro-batch approximately every 5 minutes.
The behavior: Spark will sleep and completely idle for 5 minutes. 
Every 5 minutes, it wakes up, fetches all the data accumulated over that window, processes it as a single chunk, commits a checkpoint, and goes back to sleep

3. availableNow=True
Code
query = (
    df.writeStream
      .format("delta")
      .option("checkpointLocation", "/checkpoints/customers")
      .trigger(availableNow=True)
      .toTable("silver.customers")
)
Meaning

Process the data that is available when the query starts, including backlog, and then terminate naturally.

4. once=True
Code
query = (
    df.writeStream
      .format("delta")
      .option("checkpointLocation", "/checkpoints/customers")
      .trigger(once=True)
      .toTable("silver.customers")
)
Meaning

Execute one micro-batch, then terminate.
5. Continuous Trigger
Code
query = (
    df.writeStream
      .trigger(continuous="1 second")
      .format("delta")
      .option("checkpointLocation", "/checkpoints/customers")
      .toTable("silver.customers")
)
Meaning

This uses Spark's continuous processing execution mode rather than the normal micro-batch model.
The behavior: The engine does not wait 5 minutes to process data. It processes every single record immediately as it arrives (sub-millisecond latency).
However, it will only write an asynchronous progress snapshot (checkpoint/epoch marker) to the write-ahead log once every 5 minutes.
